# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Luke places Jesus at a meal. “And it came to pass, as he went into the house of one of the chief Pharisees to eat bread on the sabbath day, that they watched him” (14:1). A man with the dropsy is before Him (14:2). “And Jesus answering spake unto the lawyers and Pharisees, saying, Is it lawful to heal on the sabbath day?” (14:3). “And they held their peace” (14:4). Jesus heals the man and lets him go, asks a second question about an ass or an ox fallen into a pit, and Luke ends the scene with “And they could not answer him again to these things” (14:6).'))
A(('p', '2.2 Where this saying stands. Luke 14:7 follows at once: “And he put forth a parable to those which were bidden, when he marked how they chose out the chief rooms; saying unto them.” Luke names who is spoken to, “those which were bidden,” and what Jesus had marked, how they “chose out the chief rooms.” After the second word, which is spoken “to him that bade him” (14:12), Luke records that “one of them that sat at meat with him” spoke (14:15), and Jesus then says, “A certain man made a great supper, and bade many” (14:16). That account is a separate parable and has its own study.'))
A(('call', 'What Luke Says Prompted the Saying',
   'No question from a hearer comes before the saying about the rooms. Luke gives a different reason: Jesus “marked how they chose out the chief rooms” (14:7). Luke does not say who chose them, how many chose, or which rooms were chosen, and this study adds nothing to that.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The setting before the scene. The chapter before has just ended with words of Jesus. “And, behold, there are last which shall be first, and there are first which shall be last” (13:30). Some Pharisees then came to Him (13:31), and the chapter closes with His words over Jerusalem and the words “Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord” (13:35). Luke gives no link between those words and the meal of chapter 14. They are reported as what stands before it.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Luke has said what Jesus was doing and sending others to do. “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). “And he sent them to preach the kingdom of God, and to heal the sick” (9:2). To the seventy He said, “The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you” (10:9). Jesus does not use the words “the kingdom of God” in the saying about the rooms or in the word to him that bade. A guest speaks them at 14:15.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives one reason for the first saying: Jesus “marked how they chose out the chief rooms” (14:7). He gives no reason for the second beyond the words “Then said he also to him that bade him” (14:12). The sayings are not free-floating teaching about meals in general. They belong to one Sabbath meal in the house of one of the chief Pharisees, and they are read there.'))
