A(('p', '3.8 What the passage contains. The text names a wedding, one who is bidden, the highest room, a more honourable man, one who bade, shame, the lowest room, worship, those who sit at meat, a dinner or a supper, friends, brethren, kinsmen, rich neighbours, a recompence, a feast, the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind, a blessing and the resurrection of the just. The action moves from a place taken, to a place given up, to a place offered, to a table opened to those who cannot repay.'))
A(('p', '3.9 What the passage leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who “thou” is beyond the one who is bidden. Jesus does not say what the wedding is or who is married. Jesus does not say who “he that bade thee” is, or who the more honourable man is. Jesus does not say what the highest room or the lowest room stands for. Jesus does not say who “him that bade him” is. Luke names the house as that of “one of the chief Pharisees” (14:1) and does not say in so many words that he is the one addressed at 14:12. Jesus does not say who the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind are beyond the words themselves. Jesus does not say what “the resurrection of the just” is, when it comes, or who the just are. The passage gives two cases, a saying, a command and its reasons, and it stops at the resurrection of the just.'))

