A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The saying about the rooms is told in Luke at a Sabbath meal in the house of one of the chief Pharisees (14:1). The Great Banquet is told in the same house after a guest says, “Blessed is he that shall eat bread in the kingdom of God” (14:15). The Wedding Banquet is told in Matthew in the temple (22:1). A shared word such as “bidden,” “wedding” or “supper” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The sayings of Luke 14:7–14 have one who is bidden, one who bade, a more honourable man, a host, and, in the command, friends, brethren, kinsmen, rich neighbours, the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind. The Great Banquet has “a certain man,” one servant and those who make excuses. The Wedding Banquet has a king, a son, servants, armies and a man without a garment. No character is carried from one into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The saying about the rooms moves from a wedding, to a place taken and given up, to a place offered, and to a saying. The word to him that bade moves from those who can repay, to those who cannot, to a recompence. The Great Banquet moves from a supper, to a sending, to excuses, to streets and lanes, to highways and hedges. None of those movements is read into the other.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, the king, the son, the armies and the wedding garment belong to Matthew 22 only, and the inspection of a guest is not read into Luke 14. Second, the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind of 14:13 are named in a command to a host. A similar list appears at 14:21 as “the poor, and the maimed, and the halt, and the blind,” and neither verse is read as explaining the other. Third, the saying that one who exalts himself shall be abased appears in 14:11, in Matthew 23:12 and in Luke 18:14 in different words and in different settings, and none of them explains another. Fourth, “the resurrection of the just” in 14:14 is read where Jesus says it and is not read from other passages that speak of resurrection.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached the kingdom of God (4:43), sent the Twelve to preach it (9:2) and told the seventy to say, “The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you” (10:9). The sayings of Luke 14:7–14 belong to that ministry. Luke places them at a Sabbath meal among lawyers, Pharisees and those bidden (14:1, 3, 7), and the Lord speaks to them there.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The Lord’s sayings at this meal were spoken before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The table, the feast and the resurrection of the just of which Jesus speaks belong to the ministry He carried out among those bidden. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The wedding, the rooms, the feast and the recompence are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The resurrection of the just is not turned into the Body’s hope. The passage is not read as a picture of how any person comes to God. Sitting down, going up, calling and recompensing are actions in a saying, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this passage: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the passage shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of humility.** “He that humbleth himself shall be exalted” (14:11). A reader learns not to make his own place the first thing he reaches for.',
    '**A lesson of patience about place.** The man who waits in the lowest room is the one called higher (14:10). A reader learns that a place given by another is better than a place taken.',
    '**A lesson of open-handed giving.** The host is told to call those who “cannot recompense” him (14:14). A reader learns to give without arranging a return.',
    '**A lesson about reward.** The recompence is placed at the resurrection of the just (14:14). A reader learns not to demand payment for a kindness at once.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s table and the resurrection of the just are not turned into the Body’s hope, and the host’s command is not turned into a requirement laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this passage.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'At a Sabbath meal in the house of one of the chief Pharisees, Jesus put forth a parable to those bidden. A man bidden to a wedding is not to sit down in the highest room, lest he begin with shame to take the lowest. He is to sit down in the lowest, and the one who bade him may say, “Friend, go up higher.” Jesus adds, “For whosoever exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.” He then says to him that bade him that a dinner or a supper is not to be made for friends, brethren, kinsmen or rich neighbours, who may bid him again, but for the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind, who cannot recompense him, and that he shall be recompensed at the resurrection of the just. Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that a place is given and not taken, that exalting is met with abasing and humbling with exalting, and that a table opened to those who cannot repay has its recompence at the resurrection of the just. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the one bidden, the rooms, the poor or the just are, or that makes sitting, calling or recompensing a way of being saved, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a parable to those bidden; a man bidden to a wedding and the highest room; the lowest room and “go up higher”; the saying about exalting and humbling; the word to him that bade; the dinner for those who can repay and the feast for those who cannot; the resurrection of the just.',
    '**The meaning:** a place is given and not taken; exalting is met with abasing and humbling with exalting; a return that can be repaid is already a return; the recompence is placed at the resurrection of the just.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the one bidden, the rooms, the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind or the just; nothing carried in from Matthew 22 or the Great Banquet.',
    '**The present lesson:** humility, patience about place, open-handed giving and patience about reward, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between hearers.** The saying about the rooms is spoken to those which were bidden (14:7). The word about the dinner is spoken to him that bade him (14:12). Luke marks the change with “Then said he also.”',
    '**Between kinds of speech.** The first is called “a parable” by Luke (14:7). The second is not given that name in the text.',
    '**Between actions.** Sitting down in the highest room, being told to give place, sitting down in the lowest, being told to go up higher, calling friends, calling the poor. Each is one action in the saying.',
    '**Between this passage and its neighbors.** The garment, the king and the son belong to Matthew 22. The excuses, the streets and lanes and the compelling belong to the Great Banquet. The two men who went up to pray belong to Luke 18.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the one bidden, the wedding, the rooms, the one who bade, the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind or the just. The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from Matthew 22, Matthew 23, Luke 18 or the Great Banquet, and no meaning of the resurrection of the just from other passages on resurrection.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Sitting, going up, calling and recompensing are actions in a saying. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

