A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Great Banquet, the Wedding Banquet, the Pharisee and the Tax Collector and the Tower and the Warring King are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. This passage makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this one.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. At a Sabbath meal in a chief Pharisee’s house, Jesus put forth a parable to those bidden. A man bidden to a wedding is not to sit down in the highest room, lest he begin with shame to take the lowest. He is to sit down in the lowest room and be told, “Friend, go up higher.” Jesus says that whosoever exalteth himself shall be abased and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted. He then tells him that bade him to call the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind, who cannot recompense him, and says that he shall be recompensed at the resurrection of the just. Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that a place is given and not taken, that exalting is met with abasing and humbling with exalting, and that a table opened to those who cannot repay has its recompence at the resurrection of the just.'))
